data.content:
Since there's no project file to confirm the target framework, here's the **lightest option** — use .NET 8+'s built-in `TimeProvider`:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In tests**, pass `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-10)); // move time back
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is lightest:**
- `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (.NET 8+) — no custom interface needed.
- The optional parameter means existing callers don't break (no DI container required).
- Test package: `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (one NuGet).

If you're on .NET 6/7, the same pattern works with a 2-line `IClock` interface instead. Want me to apply the `TimeProvider` change to your file?
